spec:
  grid: 13-15 rows x 12-18 cols
  background: random blue(1) and black(0) noise, roughly 50/50 mix
  colors_input: black, blue, green
  colors_output: adds teal(8) fill; green markers preserved
  wedges:
    count: 2-4 per grid, non-overlapping (regions separated by >=4 manhattan)
    height: each is 2 or 3; exactly one h=3 wedge per grid, rest are h=2 [observed]
    orientation: up/down/left/right; mixed orientations across wedges [observed]
    tip: a single green pixel; must not trigger valid wedge detection in other orientations
    side_markers: |
      green pixels placed on the two 45-degree diagonal rays from tip.
      Each ray has 0-h markers at distances 1..height. At least one distance d
      must have green on BOTH rays (shared distance). Some markers may be omitted
      (50-100% of in-bounds side positions filled). No extraneous green pixels.
    clipping: 0-2 wedges may have fill cells touching grid edge [observed]
  transform: fill each wedge's triangular interior (non-green cells) with teal

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick grid size (H 13-15, W 12-18).
    2. Choose 2-4 wedges with orientations and heights (one h=3, rest h=2).
    3. Place wedge tips on the grid ensuring all fill cells + side markers fit
       or are acceptably clipped, and wedge regions (fill + markers) are pairwise
       non-overlapping with >=4 manhattan gap.
    4. For each wedge, compute full side marker positions on both diagonal rays.
       Randomly omit some markers, but ensure at least one shared distance remains
       and height >= 2 is preserved by the solver's detection logic.
    5. Verify no green pixel triggers a spurious wedge in an unintended orientation.
    6. Fill background (non-green, non-teal cells) with random blue/black noise.
    7. To get input: copy output grid and replace all teal cells with the
       background color that was assigned in step 6 (i.e., remove the teal fill).
